{"question_id": "V2-dev-BOOK-en-T1-001", "approach": "LC-BM25-DOCS", "approach_base": "LC-BM25-DOCS", "flags": {}, "setting": "en-en", "q_lang": "en", "d_lang": "en", "answer_text": "Faraday explains that the cup is formed by the regular ascending current of air produced by the candle's own heat (Lecture I).\n\n- As air comes to the candle, the heat creates an upward current that cools all the sides of the wax or tallow, keeping the edge much cooler than the part within (Lecture I).\n- The part within melts because the flame runs down the wick as far as it can before being extinguished, while the outside does not melt (Lecture I).\n- If the current is disturbed in one direction, the cup becomes lop-sided and the fluid runs over, causing guttering, since gravity holds the fluid horizontal (Lecture I).\n- No fuel would serve for a candle that cannot form this cup, except material like Irish bogwood, which holds its own fuel like a sponge (Lecture I).", "abstained": false, "evidence_chars": 142899, "llm_calls": 1, "prompt_tokens": 32468, "completion_tokens": 175, "llm_wall_ms": 3459.4, "latency_ms": 7261.2, "reader_ms": 3461.8, "failed_calls": 0, "error": null, "ts": "2026-11-10T16:55:18", "models": {"chat": "openrouter/z-ai/glm-5.3-flash"}, "split": "dev", "trace": {"lc": {"arm": "LC-BM25-DOCS", "l_tok": 32000, "pack_sha256": "5471023175cfe2eb9fd00a759e77ae588cb684afe09cc009faa52fc3817d261d", "info": {"budget_chars": 142900, "ranking": [{"doc": "K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en", "score": 10.419}, {"doc": "K.gutenberg-1015_parkman_oregon-trail.en", "score": 4.137}, {"doc": "K.gutenberg-20203_franklin_autobiography.en", "score": 3.205}, {"doc": "K.gutenberg-2009_darwin_origin-of-species-6th-ed.en", "score": 2.416}, {"doc": "K.gutenberg-1228_darwin_origin-of-species-1st-ed.en", "score": 1.849}, {"doc": "K.gutenberg-815_tocqueville_democracy-in-america-part1.en", "score": 1.159}, {"doc": "K.gutenberg-833_veblen_theory-of-leisure-class.en", "score